The corrected test passes: the extracted traversal counts 24 plants + 14 bears = 38 (matching the independent `forEachCell` count), `isViable` is correct, and the population details are right. (The `Weather: null` is just the uninitialized Weather singleton in the isolated harness — unrelated to iteration.)

## Summary

I unified the duplicated full-field traversal that lived in both `FieldStats.generateCounts()` and `SimulatorView.showStatus()` by extracting it into one shared utility on `Field` — the class that owns the grid.

**`Field.java`** — new single iteration mechanism:
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface CellVisitor {
    void visit(int row, int col, Object occupant);
}

public void forEachCell(CellVisitor visitor) {
    for(int row = 0; row < depth; row++) {
        for(int col = 0; col < width; col++) {
            visitor.visit(row, col, field[row][col]);
        }
    }
}
```
It visits every cell in row-major order (including empties, passing `null`), so each caller keeps its own per-cell policy.

**`FieldStats.generateCounts()`** — the nested `for` loop became:
```java
field.forEachCell((row, col, animal) -> {
    if(animal != null) {
        incrementCount(animal.getClass());
    }
});
```

**`SimulatorView.showStatus()`** — same traversal, now:
```java
field.forEachCell((row, col, animal) -> {
    if(animal != null) {
        stats.incrementCount(animal.getClass());
        fieldView.drawMark(col, row, getColor(animal.getClass()));
    } else {
        fieldView.drawMark(col, row, EMPTY_COLOR);
    }
});
```

**Behavior preserved:** identical row-major order and identical per-cell logic — `FieldStats` only acts on non-null cells; `SimulatorView` still draws `EMPTY_COLOR` for empty cells. The grid traversal now exists in exactly one place; the two callers differ only in the callback they pass. Full codebase compiles under `javac 21` (excluding the pre-existing JUnit-dependent `SimulatorTest.java`), and a behavioral check confirms counts match.

